      "content": "hey, i'm doing some planning for a cedar deck build in vancouver. i need to walk through the basics of calculating live load vs dead load for the beam and post sizing. can u explain the difference and how they factor into the total load? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Calculating live load vs dead load for your cedar deck build in Vancouver is crucial to ensure the structural integrity of your deck. The difference between these two loads is quite simple, really. Dead load refers to the weight of the deck itself, including the decking boards, joists, beams, and posts. It's like the weight of a fully-stocked pantry, if you will. You can't just leave it there, you need to account for it in your calculations.\n\nOn the other hand, live load refers to the weight of people, furniture, and other objects that will be placed on the deck. It's like the weight of a group of rowdy party guests, if you will. You need to consider this weight to ensure your deck can support the expected loads.\n\nWhen calculating the total load, you need to add the dead load to the live load. This is often referred to as the total load or total weight. For example, if your deck has a dead load of 10 pounds per square foot and a live load of 40 pounds per square foot, the total load would be 50 pounds per square foot.\n\nNow, when it comes to sizing your beams and posts, you need to consider the total load and the fact",
