      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for the live load, is there a specific psi or psf standard i should be looking at for residential code here in BC? and also, what are some of the common things that cause unexpected or irregular weight distributions that i should watch out for when i'm planning the layout?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In BC, the residential code typically uses a live load of 40 psf (pounds per square foot) for most decks, but it can vary depending on the specific code requirements and the type of deck. For example, the BC Building Code requires a live load of 30 psf for decks with a roof, while a deck without a roof can use 40 psf.\n\nAs for unexpected or irregular weight distributions, there are several things to watch out for. One common issue is the \"gathering point\" effect, where people tend to congregate in the center of the deck, creating an uneven distribution of weight. This can be mitigated by placing furniture and other heavy objects towards the edges of the deck.\n\nAnother issue is the \" furniture placement\" problem, where people tend to place heavy furniture, such as patio sets or BBQs, in the same spot every time. This can create a pattern of weight distribution that's not evenly distributed across the deck.\n\nYou should also consider the \"garden gnome\" effect, where people tend to place small, heavy objects, such as garden gnomes or decorative stones, in a pattern that creates an uneven weight distribution. And, of course, there's the \"party animal\"",
