    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"garden gnome\" effect is a colloquialism I like to use to describe the tendency of people to place small, heavy objects in a pattern that can create an uneven weight distribution on a deck. It's a bit of a humorous way to acknowledge that people often don't think about the structural implications of their decorating choices.\n\nAs for the \"party animal\" reference, I was thinking of the fact that people often tend to get a bit rowdy when they're on a deck, especially if they've had a few drinks. This can lead to unexpected weight distributions, such as people standing on the edge of the deck or placing heavy objects in a way that creates an uneven load. It's not necessarily a structural concern, but it's something to keep in mind when designing a deck that will be used by a large number of people.\n\nIn terms of actual weight limits, it's worth noting that the BC Building Code requires that decks be designed to support a minimum of 50 psf for most residential applications. However, this can vary depending on the specific code requirements and the type of deck. It's always a good idea to consult with a structural engineer or a qualified builder to determine the specific weight limits for your deck.",
